# Check Reachability BEFORE Fixing, Not After

When you find and fix a bug that appears at multiple call sites of a shared
function (or any multi-location fix), grep for every actual caller of each
affected function **before** editing it — as part of the investigation, not
as a follow-up check afterward.

For each site you're about to touch, answer: is this function actually
called by anything in a live code path, or is it dead/orphaned code that
nothing currently invokes?

This changes:
- **Risk assessment** — a fix to dead code is zero-risk and can be
  deprioritized or mentioned as "inert until something calls it."
- **What you tell the user** — "this fixes 6 call sites" is a different,
  weaker claim than "this fixes 4 live call sites; 2 aren't reachable by
  anything today." Say the second, more precise version.

## Bad (discovered reachability only after being asked to double-check)

Map every place a value gets *written*, fix all of them, tell the user
"all fixed" — then only check whether each site is actually called by
anything when the user later asks "are you sure this is safe?"

## Good

While mapping the fix sites, also grep for callers of each function
(`functionName\(` across the codebase). Note in the plan/summary which
sites are live vs. orphaned *before* asking the user to approve the fix,
not after.

## Why

Skipping this step means confidently claiming safety/coverage without
having actually verified it — the exact gap that erodes trust even when
the underlying fix is fine.
